<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitrov</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitrov is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 65 kilometres north of Moscow. It was founded in 1154 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a>, who named it after the patron saint of his newborn son Vsevolod, baptised Dmitry. Its earthen ramparts and 16th-century Cathedral of the Dormition survive, and it later served as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of Moscow princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitrov.jpg" alt="Dmitrov" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike1979 Russia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitrov_Church_of_the_Dormition_2023-09-23_8718.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dmitrov lies 65 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, on the Yakhroma River and the Moscow Canal, at an altitude of 179 metres. It is the administrative centre of Dmitrovsky District in Moscow Oblast and one of the oldest urban areas in the oblast. Its name means Dmitri's, and it is explained by the fact that the patron saint of Vsevolod, the son of Yury Dolgoruky born where the town was founded, was Saint Demetrius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Yury Dolgoruky founded the town in 1154. In the 13th century the settlement marked the point where the borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>, Tver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> met. It belonged to the princes of Galich-Mersky, who lived far to the north, until 1364, when it was incorporated into the Grand Duchy of Moscow. Dmitry Donskoy and his grandson Vasily II each granted Dmitrov as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to a younger son, so that it became the capital of a tiny principality. It received town rights in 1374.</p><p>The reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>'s son Yury Ivanovich, from 1503 to 1533, began the golden age of the town. During it the black-domed Assumption Cathedral in the kremlin and a smaller monastery cathedral of Sts. Boris and Gleb were built. The town then passed to Yury's brother Andrey of Staritsa. In 1569 it was seized from Vladimir of Staritsa and added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oprichnina">Oprichnina</a>, and it declined afterward. It suffered further damage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> ransacked it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> briefly occupied Dmitrov in 1812. The anarchist prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a> spent his last years there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>. Soviet archaeologists excavated the local kremlin in the 1930s. In November 1941 German troops occupied the town and crossed the Moscow-Volga Canal from there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dmitrov had a population of 65,574 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a railway junction, on the branch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s Savyolovsky terminal to Savyolovo and the branch to Alexandrov, and a cargo port on the Moscow Canal. The kremlin's Assumption Cathedral recalls the town's golden age.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dmitrov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yuri Dolgoruky, the Long-armed, founded or fortified towns such as Yuryev-Polsky, Pereslavl-Zalessky and <b>Dmitrov</b>, and Moscow is first mentioned in 1147 as the place of a feast he held.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rostov-suzdal">Principality of Rostov-Suzdal</a></li></ul></section>
